    "history.era1_badge": "Batch · TShark",
    "history.era1_caption": "PS3 Blacklist Sniffer v2.1.1 — Windows CMD & TShark (Jan 2022)",
    "history.era1_h1": "Live PSN username detection directly from game network traffic",
    "history.era1_h2": "Windows desktop & jailbroken PS3 webMAN MOD notifications",
    "history.era1_h3": "25 versions over 6 months (94 commits) laying the foundation for P2P tracking",
    "history.era1_period": "Nov 2021 – May 2022 · v1.0 → v2.2.4",
    "history.era1_summary": "Where Session Sniffer began: built originally as a personal defense against mod-menu crashers, DDoSers, and IP booters on PlayStation 3 across GTA V, GTA IV, Red Dead Redemption, Minecraft, and Call of Duty. This Windows Batch script used TShark to watch live network traffic for blacklisted PSN usernames, triggering instant Windows & webMAN MOD PS3 alerts so you could leave before getting crashed or booted.",
    "history.era1_title": "PS3 Blacklist Sniffer",
    "history.era2_badge": "Python Console · v1.0",
    "history.era2_caption": "GTA V Session Sniffer v1.0.0 — First Python Console Release (Jan 10, 2023)",
    "history.era2_h1": "First full Python console app showing all connected & disconnected players",
    "history.era2_h2": "Early MaxMind GeoLite2 country resolution & per-peer port tracking",
    "history.era2_h3": "Worked on PC, Xbox One, and PlayStation without modifying game files",
    "history.era2_period": "Jan 2023 – Feb 2024 · v1.0.0 → v1.0.6",
    "history.era2_summary": "Launched on January 10, 2023 alongside PC Blacklist Sniffer, this first Python rewrite happened thanks to my legendary friend {grub4k}, who kindly taught me Python in our free time so I could move on from the slowness and clunkiness of Batch to a much faster language. After having a script that only warned about specific usernames and IPs met in-game, I wanted to display the whole session in my GTA V lobbies — simply because it would be cool, for monitoring and curiosity, and to kill dead time while AFKing in-game and coding. GTA V Session Sniffer v1.0 tracked every connected and disconnected peer in real time with First/Last Seen timestamps, packet counters, ports, and MaxMind GeoLite2 country lookups — without game mods.",
    "history.era2_title": "PC Blacklist & GTA V Session Sniffer v1.0",
    "history.era3_badge": "Console Tables · v1.1 – v1.2",
    "history.era3_caption": "GTA V Session Sniffer v1.1.8 — Live Console Tables & Host Crown (Nov 6, 2024)",
    "history.era3_h1": "Automatic MaxMind GeoLite2 updater & 1,500 IPs/min batch IP-API enrichment",
    "history.era3_h2": "Local network (ARP) scanning, Mobile / VPN / Hosting flags & Session Host crown",
    "history.era3_h3": "Sortable console tables, Rejoins / traffic rate metrics & Discord Rich Presence",
    "history.era3_period": "Mar 2024 – Dec 2024 · v1.1.0 → v1.2.9 (20 Releases)",
    "history.era3_summary": "2024 was when I truly fell in love with working on and improving this project — coding it literally became fun and a joy to build. That momentum transformed the script into a full intelligence terminal: automatic GeoLite2 database downloads, LAN ARP scanning, 1,500 IPs/min batch IP-API lookups, PrettyTable color-coded connected/disconnected views, Rejoins & PPS metrics, Mobile/VPN/Hosting detection, Session Host crown tracking, and Discord Rich Presence all arrived.",
    "history.era3_title": "Live Console Tables, Auto-GeoLite2 & Local Network Scan",
    "history.era4_badge": "PyQt6 GUI · v1.3 – v1.4",
    "history.era4_caption": "Session Sniffer v1.3.0 — First PyQt6 Desktop GUI (Dec 2024 / Jan 2025)",
    "history.era4_h1": "First native PyQt6 + QDarkStyle GUI with sortable tables & right-click menus",
    "history.era4_h2": "Renamed to universal \"Session Sniffer\" with standalone Windows .exe builds",
    "history.era4_h3": "Ping tools, collapsible tables, Bandwidth stats, voice alerts & detailed logs",
    "history.era4_period": "Dec 2024 – Jan 2026 · v1.3.0 → v1.4.3 (63 Releases)",
    "history.era4_summary": "The arrival of AI is what immediately pushed me to opt for a Python GUI — and I'm not ashamed to admit it. Sticking with Python was an easy call: the entire source code and backend were already built and rock-solid, and all I needed was a graphical interface on top. On December 15, 2024, the terminal evolved into a native PyQt6 desktop app, and on January 4, 2025 the project dropped \"GTA V\" to become universal Session Sniffer — adding standalone .exe builds, right-click context menus, ICMP/TCP/spoofed Ping, Wireshark MAC lookups, collapsible tables, bandwidth metrics, spoken voice alerts, and ARP spoofing.",
    "history.era4_title": "First Qt Desktop GUI & \"Session Sniffer\"",
    "history.era5_badge": "PySide6 · Native libpcap · v1.5",
    "history.era5_caption": "Session Sniffer v1.5.0 — Modern Cross-Platform PySide6 Suite (Nov 2026)",
    "history.era5_h1": "PySide6 UI + native libpcap/Npcap/BPF engine (no TShark required) on Windows, Linux & macOS",
    "history.era5_h2": "RDR2 feature set, Looky System, Live Graphs, Port Scanner, Hotspot & LAN Web Dashboard",
    "history.era5_h3": "6 built-in themes + 20-color Theme Manager & 33 languages including right-to-left scripts",
    "history.era5_period": "Jan 2026 – Nov 2026 · v1.5.0 (2,100+ Commits, 73 Releases)",
    "history.era5_summary": "The largest overhaul in the project's history — and still proudly built in Python. Even with AI, I never wanted to build a sniffer in another language I don't know: I know Python, so I code in Python, I refuse to be blind to the code AI generates, and I want fine control to stay completely in charge — that's what modern-day programming is now, at least for me. Re-architected into a modular PySide6 suite with native libpcap/Npcap/BPF capture (eliminating TShark) across Windows, Linux, and macOS, it added RDR2 tools, Looky System, Live Graphs, Detections & Combo Rules, Embedded LAN Web Panel with QR Code, Wi-Fi Hotspot Manager, 6 themes + Theme Manager, and 33 languages with right-to-left support.",
    "history.era5_title": "Cross-Platform Analysis Suite",
